      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\), we start by recalling a property of determinants for square matrices. Specifically, the determinant of a matrix \\(\\mathbf{N}\\) raised to the power \\(k\\) is the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{N}\\) raised to the power \\(k\\) for any square matrix \\(\\mathbf{N}\\) and any integer \\(k\\). \\(\\det(\\mathbf{N}^k) = (\\det \\mathbf{N})^k\\).\n\nIn this problem, we have \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\). According to the property mentioned above, we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^4\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\), we substitute \\(-2\\) for \\(\\det \\mathbf{M}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (-2)^4\n\\]\n\nComputing the power of \\(-2\\):\n\n\\[\n(-2)^4 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
